[00:00.00]All right, everyone, let's dive into this practice sheet together. This is a great way to work on completing sentences, answering questions, and even creating a little dialogue, which is super useful for improving our language skills. First, let's look at the section where we're completing sentences. Here you see sentences that need a bit of help to be complete. For example, you play. Your task is to think of something you could play, like soccer or the guitar, to fill in the blank. On the right side, you have to complete similar sentences, but these use negatives like don't or don't want. For example, I don't want could be something you might not be interested in having or doing, like I don't want broccoli. Now, moving on to answering the questions. These are straightforward questions that you can answer based on your personal experiences. For instance, do you like to play soccer? If you do, you can answer, yes, I do. Or if not, you can say, no, I don't. Remember, it's all about practice, so take your time to think about what your honest answer would be. Lastly, we have a fun section where you create a dialogue between John and Ben. This is your chance to get creative. Imagine what John might say to start a conversation, what Ben would reply, and how John could respond back. It's a bit like writing a mini script for a play, and it helps you think about how conversations flow in real life. So, take your time with each section, be creative, and most importantly, have fun with it. Practice makes perfect, and you're doing great.